## Landing

How `/ship-plan` lands a reviewed plan. `integrate`: `pull-request` pushes the plan's branch and opens a pull request; `direct` pushes to the default branch. `release`: `none`; `tag` tags the plan's `Release:` version, or the next free one when another plan landed it first, and a plan has that line only when it changes something a release ships, such as the product's code, its package's readme or its build, so one that changes only site pages, CI, decision records, tests or repository docs lands with no tag and no release (ADR-0032 in `docs/decisions/project-template/`); when this project's releases ship something else, say what under `## Conventions`; `tag+release` also publishes a release from that version's section of `changelog`. On GitHub, `.github/workflows/tag-release.yml` tags and publishes it as the pull request merges, from the `Release:` line `/ship-plan` writes in its description or the changelog's newest section, and for a .NET library starts `.github/workflows/publish.yml`; `/ship-plan` run after the merge does only what that workflow didn't. `changelog`: the file every plan's last step writes, in the Keep a Changelog format, `CHANGELOG.md` by default. `pre-release`: `untracked` keeps the project pre-release until a version tag of 1.0.0 or later: nothing it released or stored has a reader that must keep working, so every text describes the product as it is, the changelog's `## [Unreleased]` is a catalog of what the product does that each plan edits in place, and a breaking change needs no deprecation or migration path, as ADR-0015 in `docs/decisions/project-template/` says; `tracked`, the default when the line is missing, tracks every change from the first release, for a project whose releases or stored data already have readers. `draft`: `no`, the default when the line is missing, which a template update never changes, so a project lands as it did; `yes` opens every pull request as a draft, as `/ship-plan draft` does,{% if git_host == 'github' and code_review is defined and code_review %} and a new project that runs NCode Review starts with it, since its approval lets a ready pull request merge once a second person has write access, so marking it ready is the person's review (ADR-0022 and ADR-0034 in `docs/decisions/project-template/`);{% endif %} and `/run-issues` opens its pull requests as drafts either way. `follow-ups`: `issues` files a plan's follow-ups as issues in this repository; `<owner/repo>` files them in that repository; `none` files none, and `/ship-plan` lists them for you to record. `follow-up-label`: the label those issues get, `follow-up` by default; write a name with spaces as it is, such as `tech debt`. `gate`: `none`; a command, such as `./build/dod.ps1 -Fast`, that `/ship-plan`, `/run-issues`, `/address-feedback` and `/run-feedback` run from the repository root before each push, stopping when it exits non-zero or changes a file. `protect`: `none`; `hook` makes `.githooks/pre-push` refuse any push to the default branch, for a host that can't require pull requests, such as a private repository on GitHub Free. `pr-format`: `none`; `<file>#<heading>`, such as `AGENTS.md#Pull requests`, names the section that sets the title and description of the pull requests `/ship-plan` opens, which still end with their `Closes` lines. `review-bots`: the review bots, by login and separated by commas, whose findings `/run-issues`, `/address-feedback` and `/run-feedback` may fix, `ncode-review` by default, or `none`; `/run-issues` and `/run-feedback` judge every one of NCode Review's findings, whatever its severity, and fix each that holds, and a bot's finding alone never changes CI, the template, agent configuration, the build or the dependencies: `/address-feedback` and `/run-feedback` change them only when you approve it in their start question. `max-review-rounds`: `3`; after that many rounds of review feedback on a pull request, `/run-issues` and `/run-feedback` comment no further `/review`, so NCode Review isn't asked to review again, and still fix and answer every finding; they recommend the merge once every finding is fixed, declined or answered and the review of the last push they asked for is in. `prelude`: `none`; one PowerShell command that sets up the shell the workflow's commands run in, such as `./build/env.ps1`, a script in the repository, or `nvs use 26`, to put a tool installed through a version manager on `PATH`. `/ship-plan`, `/run-issues`, `/address-feedback` and `/run-feedback` run it before the gate, and executors and coordinators before each Done-when command, dot-sourced in the same PowerShell; a plan names it under Context for the executor, so no step repeats it. When it fails, they stop and show its error. Write it on one line, without a leading `. ` or `& ` operator, a `#` comment, or `;`, `|`, `&`, `&&` or `||`; `.template/Get-PlanState.ps1` prints any other value as `prelude=invalid`, and `/ship-plan` stops on it. `/run-issues` runs only when the host requires pull requests into the default branch, or `protect` is `hook` and the hooks are enabled.

- integrate: pull-request
- release: {{ release_policy }}
- changelog: CHANGELOG.md
- pre-release: {{ pre_release }}
- draft: {% if git_host == 'github' and code_review is defined and code_review %}yes{% else %}no{% endif %}
- follow-ups: issues
- follow-up-label: follow-up
- gate: {% if stack == 'dotnet' %}./build/dod.ps1 -Fast{% elif stack == 'node' and node_lint is defined and node_lint %}if (Test-Path package.json) { {{ package_manager }} run lint }{% else %}none{% endif %}
- protect: none
- pr-format: none
- review-bots: ncode-review
- max-review-rounds: 3
- prelude: {% if uses_node %}./.template/Use-Node.ps1{% else %}none{% endif %}

